  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This Dryad dataset shows early but clear scholarly and reuse activity: it has 70 recorded downloads, 421 views, 1 citation in OpenAlex, and 2 documented downstream reuse signals. The record suggests the dataset has been noticed within ecology and conservation research, while overall uptake remains modest and still developing.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This dataset accompanies research on how hunting, exotic carnivores, and habitat loss shape native carnivore communities in Madagascar, a topic closely aligned with carnivore ecology, occupancy, habitat change, and wildlife conservation. Its strongest indicators are stewardship and use: the dataset has been viewed 421 times and downloaded 70 times in Dryad, and it is connected to downstream use through both a citing article and additional reuse signals.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation record is still small, with 1 OpenAlex citation, so the scholarly footprint is best described as emerging rather than established. The citing work appeared in 2021 as a preprint from an ecology-focused research context, indicating that the dataset is already feeding into related work on land use, biodiversity, and conservation questions.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Beyond formal citation, the reuse graph identifies 2 documented downstream reuses, including the related article record and a Europe PMC article mention. This points to some broader visibility of the dataset in the literature ecosystem, even though the volume of reuse remains limited.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There is no recorded online mention activity in the available public-discourse sources, and the data record does not show evidence of broader media attention. Taken together, the dataset\u2019s impact profile is one of focused scholarly relevance: openly available, used by others, and already contributing to conservation-oriented research, but still at an early stage of accumulation.</p>",
